.TH YEW-PKG 1 "2026-10-12" "yew" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yew-pkg \- install and maintain Fletch plugins for yew
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yew pkg install
.I SPEC
.RB [ \-\-rev " R" |
.BR \-\-tag " T" |
.BR \-\-branch " B" ]
.RB [ \-\-enable ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout " SECONDS" ]
.br
.B yew pkg update
.RI [ NAME ...]
.RB [ \-\-dry-run ]
.RB [ \-\-discard-local ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout " SECONDS" ]
.br
.B yew pkg remove
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-keep-grants ]
.br
.B yew pkg list
.RI [ NAME ...]
.RB [ \-\-long ]
.br
.B yew pkg doctor
.RI [ NAME ...]
.RB [ \-\-fix | \-\-accept ]
.br
.B yew pkg verify
.RI [ NAME ...]
.RB [ \-\-fix | \-\-accept ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yew pkg
installs and maintains Fletch plugins from Git repositories.
It is a command-line operation; editor startup never runs Git or checks for
updates.
An installation clones into a temporary directory, validates the pure-literal
.I plugin.fl
manifest, checks out a detached revision, records the revision and a content
tree hash, and atomically moves the directory into place.
The plugin remains disabled unless installation used
.BR \-\-enable .
.PP
There is no central registry, namespace ownership, signing, review,
dependency resolution, build hook, install hook, submodule recursion, or
automatic update in yew 1.0.
Plugins are Fletch-only and declare no dependencies.
The rejected commands
.B publish
and
.B search
explain the absence of a registry.
Network operations require Git 2.24 or newer and use a 60-second timeout by
default.
.SH SOURCE SPECIFICATIONS
The accepted source forms are closed:
.TP
.BI gh: user/repo
Resolve through
.BR https://github.com/ .
.TP
.BI gl: user/repo
Resolve through
.BR https://gitlab.com/ .
.TP
.BI cb: user/repo
Resolve through
.BR https://codeberg.org/ .
.TP
.BI sr: ~user/repo
Resolve through
.BR https://git.sr.ht/ .
.TP
.BR https:// , " " http:// , " " ssh:// , " " git://
Use the URL verbatim.
.TP
.BI git@ host:path
Use the scp-like source verbatim.
.TP
.IR /absolute , " " ./relative , " " file://...
Use the local source verbatim.
.PP
A bare
.I user/repo
is rejected because silently assuming a hosting site would create an implicit
registry.
Refs use
.B [A-Za-z0-9._/-]{1,255}
and may neither begin with a minus nor contain a
.B ..
path component.
User-controlled Git arguments follow
.B \-\-end-of-options
or
.BR \-\- .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS install
.B yew pkg install
performs a full clone, resolves the requested ref, checks it out detached,
reads the manifest without executing it, hashes the content, and installs it
under the manifest's name.
There is no name override.
.PP
Exactly one of the following pins may be supplied:
.TP
.BI \-\-rev " R"
Pin the resolved 40-hex commit permanently.
Future updates perform no network operation for this entry.
.TP
.BI \-\-tag " T"
Pin a tag name.
A moved tag is detected and is not trusted silently.
.TP
.BI \-\-branch " B"
Follow a branch, subject to the fast-forward-only update rule.
.TP
.B \-\-enable
Record the new plugin as enabled.
Without this option it is installed disabled.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
Set a positive network timeout in seconds.
.TP
.B \-\-force-relock
Permit replacement of a corrupt lock file during installation.
This explicitly discards the revisions and tree hashes that could not be
read.
.PP
With no pin option the entry follows the remote default branch as
.BR head .
Installation executes nothing from the repository.
A repository containing
.I .gitmodules
produces a warning; the rejected
.B \-\-recurse-submodules
option explains why yew will not expand that trust surface.
.SS update
With no names,
.B update
visits every lock entry in deterministic order.
Revision pins stay fixed.
Head, branch, and tag pins fetch and resolve their target, then update only
when the recorded commit is an ancestor of the target.
yew never merges and never invokes
.BR "git pull" .
.PP
Before checkout yew prints up to 50 commit-subject rows.
.B \-\-dry-run
stops after this report and changes neither the checkout nor lock file.
A dirty tracked worktree is refused unless
.B \-\-discard-local
explicitly permits discarding those changes.
That option does not weaken the ancestry rule.
.B \-\-timeout
sets a positive network timeout in seconds.
.PP
A force-pushed branch or moved tag is refused with the old and proposed
revisions and instructions for inspecting the repository.
An unreachable remote does not prevent later entries from being checked;
the final summary counts updated, current, and unreachable packages.
.SS remove
.B remove
accepts only a package recorded in the lock file.
It verifies that the resolved target lies below the managed plugin root
before deleting anything and never follows symlinks inside the package.
The default removes the enabled state and persisted capability decisions.
.B \-\-keep-grants
retains those trust records.
.SS list
.B list
hashes local trees without network access.
With no names it reports every managed package; names restrict the report.
The compact output is deterministic and tab-separated:
.nf
.RS
NAME  PIN  REV12  STATE  URL
.RE
.fi
States include
.BR ok ,
.BR drift ,
.BR missing ,
and
.BR unmanaged .
.B \-\-long
adds detail while retaining deterministic order.
.SS "doctor and verify"
.B verify
is an alias of
.BR doctor .
They check named packages, or every managed package, and exit zero only when
all required checks pass.
Checks cover the lock format, directory presence, manifest validity and
identity, tree hash, repository metadata, checked-out revision, and declared
versus granted capabilities.
.PP
.B \-\-fix
restores missing or drifted state from the locked revision.
.B \-\-accept
keeps local reality and updates the lock record.
They are mutually exclusive.
Inspect a drift report before choosing either action.
.SH THE LOCK FILE
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/yew/plugins.lock
is pure-literal Fletch data with schema 1.
It cannot execute code.
An absent file means there are no managed packages; a corrupt file is an
error and is never overwritten implicitly.
Unknown top-level and per-package keys survive a rewrite.
.PP
Each package records these fields:
.TP
.B url
The resolved source supplied to Git.
.TP
.B shorthand
The original source spelling, or an empty string.
.TP
.B rev
The checked-out commit as exactly 40 lowercase hexadecimal digits.
.TP
.B pin
One of
.BR rev:<commit> ,
.BR tag:<name> ,
.BR branch:<name> ,
or
.BR head .
.TP
.B tree
A 16-hex content-tree value.
.TP
.BR installed_at ", " updated_at
Unix timestamps for the initial installation and most recent update.
.PP
Serialization is stable: top-level keys are
.B schema
then
.BR plugins ;
package names sort bytewise; fields use the order above.
To reproduce an install, copy the lock file into the same XDG data root and
install each recorded URL at its
.B rev
pin.
The lock file itself is not a command and there is no
.B yew pkg lock
subcommand.
.SH INTEGRITY
The tree value covers every regular file and symlink below the package except
the top-level
.I .git/
subtree.
Paths sort bytewise.
Regular files contribute path, executable-bit class, length, and bytes;
symlinks contribute path and target and are never followed.
.PP
The value is FNV-1a-64.
.B It is not a cryptographic hash.
It detects accidental and casual drift but does not authenticate an author,
repository, revision, tag, lock file, or crafted collision.
When load-time verification detects changed code, yew warns and revokes
persisted grants before executing the plugin.
.SH CAPABILITIES AND TRUST
An enabled plugin may request
.BR fs ,
.BR shell ,
.BR net ,
and
.BR clipboard .
The manifest declares the requested set; the user answers at first use, and
the decision is stored in
.IR trust.fl .
A denial is a catchable Fletch error.
Headless editor runs may use the repeatable top-level option
.BI \-\-grant " NAME:CAP"
to make an explicit session grant without a prompt.
.SS "The honest paragraph"
A plugin runs as Fletch in the same VM, process, and address space as the
editor.
There is no memory isolation and no resource isolation: an enabled plugin can
read any open buffer, burn CPU, or allocate until the process exhausts its
resources.
Capability gates cover the Fletch API's file, shell, network, and clipboard
surfaces and nothing else.
Enabling a plugin is an act of trust in its author; capabilities limit blast
radius, but
.B this is not a sandbox.
Read the source before enabling it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/yew/plugins/
Managed checkouts.
The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.local/share/yew/plugins/ .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/yew/plugins.lock
The pure-literal lock file.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/yew/trust.fl
Plugin enabled state and persisted capability decisions.
The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.local/state/yew/trust.fl .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Select the managed package and lock-file root.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Select the trust database root.
.TP
.B YEW_RUNTIME_DIR
Select shipped runtime files used by Fletch imports.
.PP
Git children receive noninteractive prompting and pager settings.
yew removes inherited repository-redirection variables such as
.BR GIT_DIR ,
.BR GIT_WORK_TREE ,
and
.B GIT_INDEX_FILE
before spawning Git, but retains the user's Git configuration, proxy,
credential helper, and URL rewrites.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The operation completed and every required doctor check passed.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, explicit refusal, dirty or non-fast-forward update, or lock-file
integrity error.
.TP
.B 3
Filesystem I/O, missing or old Git, network, authentication, or timeout
failure.
.TP
.B 4
An internal yew invariant failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yew (1),
.BR yew-fl (1),
.BR git (1)
